+For the normal-shock branch considered here, the distribution is invariant under transverse reflections and interchange of the two transverse velocity coordinates. Reflection makes every off-diagonal moment integrand odd, so those components vanish; interchange gives $R^{\cl}_{yy}=R^{\cl}_{zz}$. The tensor is trace-free by its defining kernel, hence $R^{\cl}_{xx}+2R^{\cl}_{yy}=0$ and $R^{\cl}_{yy}=R^{\cl}_{zz}=-R^{\cl}_{xx}/2$. Appendix~\ref{app:symmetry_proof} gives the componentwise proof and its discrete-grid conditions.
+
+These reductions use symmetries in \emph{velocity space}, not merely dependence on one spatial coordinate. A distribution $f(x,c_x,c_y,c_z)$ can vary only with $x$ while having different transverse velocity populations. Dependence on $x$ alone imposes neither $R^{\cl}_{yy}=R^{\cl}_{zz}$ nor vanishing off-diagonal components. Under the stated normal-shock symmetries, one scalar function, $R^{\cl}_{xx}(x)$, therefore specifies this tensor. The condition $R^{\cl}_{xx}+R^{\cl}_{yy}+R^{\cl}_{zz}=0$ relates its three diagonal components only: it contains no $\Delta$ and supplies no equation for that separate scalar moment. In particular, $\Delta$ is the excess of the scalar moment $\int|\boldsymbol c|^4f\,\dd\boldsymbol v$, not the trace of $R^{\cl}_{ij}$. The isotropic-mixture example in appendix~\ref{app:symmetry_proof} explicitly shows that a zero tensorial moment can coexist with a non-zero scalar excess.
+\end{revision}
